        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "e9a6be75-6930-59d1-8f7d-1b66a087e607",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Topics: Hyperaccumulators, Caesium-137\n- Concepts: Radionuclides, Hydrocarbons, Organic solvents, Inorganic solvents, Radioactive isotopes, Nuclear fission, Fission products\n- Substances: Cadmium, Caesium-137, Cobalt, Plutonium-238, Radium, Strontium, Uranium-234, Uranium-235, Uranium-238, Benzene, BTEX, DDT, Dieldrin, Endosulfan, Fluoranthene, MTBE, PCB, PCNB, TCE, Potassium ferrocyanide\n\nFacts:\n- The hyperaccumulators list covers plant species that accumulate or tolerate radionuclides, hydrocarbons and organic solvents, and inorganic solvents.\n- The radionuclides in the hyperaccumulators list include cadmium, caesium-137, cobalt, plutonium-238, radium, strontium, and uranium-234, uranium-235, and uranium-238.\n- The hydrocarbons and organic solvents in the hyperaccumulators list include benzene, BTEX, DDT, dieldrin, endosulfan, fluoranthene, MTBE, PCB, PCNB, TCE, and TCE by-products.\n- The inorganic solvents in the hyperaccumulators list include potassium ferrocyanide.\n- Caesium-137, also called Cs, Cs-137, cesium-137, or radiocaesium, is a radioactive isotope of caesium.\n- Caesium-137 is formed as a common fission product by the nuclear fission of uranium-235 and other fissionable isotopes in nuclear reactors and nuclear weapons.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6a4a7de3-7e2d-5005-8baa-4c51bab16290",
        "text": "Page: Hyperaccumulators table – 3. Sentence: This list covers hyperaccumulators, plant species which accumulate, or are tolerant of radionuclides (Cd, Cs-137, Co, Pu-238, Ra, Sr, U-234, 235, 238), hydrocarbons and organic solvents (Benzene, BTEX, DDT, Dieldrin, Endosulfan, Fluoranthene, MTBE, PCB, PCNB, TCE and by-products), and inorganic solvents (Potassium ferrocyanide).\nPage: Caesium-137. Sentence: Caesium-137 ( Cs , Cs-137), cesium-137, or radiocaesium, is a radioactive isotope of caesium which is formed as one of the more common fission products by the nuclear fission of uranium-235 and other fissionable isotopes in nuclear reactors and nuclear weapons.",
